! `i-atom-soc.in` run under LDA, which is the only way the **ultracell** can
! reach a spin-orbit dataset at all.
!
! `I.rel-pbe-nc-dojo.UPF` is the one fully-relativistic *norm-conserving* set
! committed here, and its header says PBE -- which the ultracell refuses,
! because the gradient of an ultracell density carries the envelope's own
! gradient and a per-cell evaluation drops it. `input_dft = 'LDA'` overrides the
! header, which is QE's own rule and this code's.
!
! **A dataset generated for one functional and run under another is an
! inconsistency, and it is deliberate here**: what this cell is for is a *null*
! -- the ultracell against its own unit-cell SCF, which uses the same
! pseudopotential under the same functional -- rather than a comparison against
! `pw.x`. The inconsistency is a constant on both sides and cancels. Do not use
! this file for a benchmark comparison; `i-atom-soc.in` is that one.
!
! What it covers that nothing else does: spin-orbit coupling inside an
! ultracell, which is the regime the method adds no code for. SOC is a nonlocal
! term in the Hamiltonian the frozen states were diagonalised with, so the
! ultracell only ever sees their eigenvalues and coefficients -- and this is the
! run that says so rather than the argument.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 1, celldm(1) = 12.0, nat = 1, ntyp = 1, ecutwfc = 45.0,
    occupations = 'fixed', nbnd = 8, nosym = .true., noinv = .true.,
    input_dft = 'LDA',
    noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true.,
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.5, angle1(1) = 0.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10, mixing_beta = 0.3
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 I 126.9 I.rel-pbe-nc-dojo.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
 I 0.00 0.00 0.00
K_POINTS (automatic)
 1 1 1 0 0 0
